// BorrowCheck — **E3070: A COLLECTION MAY NOT BE MUTATED WHILE AN ELEMENT BORROWED OUT OF IT IS
// STILL LIVE.**
//
// `arr.get(i)` hands the caller the element POINTER and takes no reference for it
// (`Parser.emitArrayElementAccessor(owned: false)`) — the array stays the sole owner and its
// `__arr_decref` walk destroys the element. So a write that shortens or replaces the array
// (`clear`, `push` past capacity, a rebind of the binding itself) frees a record the caller is
// still holding: measured **0xC0000005** on `let s = try arr.get(0) otherwise ""` + `arr.clear()`
// + `print("{s}")`, and on five further shapes of the same statement.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THIS IS THE SAME WRITE DOORS THE ITERATION LOCK ALREADY HARDENED (P1.8 slice A), ASKING A
// SECOND QUESTION OF THE SAME BINDING.** The lock asks *"is this binding being iterated?"*; the
// borrow set asks *"is an element of it still referenced?"*. Both are answered from ONE derivation
// of **which storage a write goes through** — `Parser.iterationSubjectNameAt` — so a chain shape
// taught to one is taught to the other, and a write cannot be locked under one key and checked
// under another. That divergence was a segfault once already; see that function's ⚠ block.
//
// ⭐ **WHERE THIS DEPARTS FROM BOTH REFERENCES, AND WHY IT HAD TO.** `BorrowCheckPass.cs` and
// `MaxonBorrowCheck.maxon` are the same algorithm, and both key a borrow on a VAR SLOT recovered
// from `varDecl`/`varLoad`/`varStore` ops. **shv2 has none of those.** `let`/`var` bind a name to
// the initializer's SSA `ValueId` in `Scope` at PARSE TIME (ARCHITECTURE.md, *Names, SSA, and
// phis*), so `let x = 42` mints no op at all: an IR pass cannot see the bindings because the
// bindings are not in the IR. The facts are therefore RECORDED BY THE PARSER, which is the only
// place they exist, and DECIDED here, which is the only place the whole-program parameter-mutation
// summary exists.
//
// Three consequences, each one an improvement on the references rather than a workaround:
//
//   • **Liveness is EXACT, not approximated.** Both references stand in "the highest physical op
//     index this slot appeared at" for the last use. shv2 needs no stand-in: a use of a binding IS
//     a `Scope` resolution (`Parser.requireBindingLive`, the one read chokepoint), and the token
//     cursor is monotone in source order through a function body — the very order the references
//     were approximating.
//   • **`pop`/`remove` are excluded STRUCTURALLY, not by name.** The mint sits at the one door that
//     already decides borrow-vs-move-out (`emitArrayElementAccessor`'s `owned` flag), so v1's
//     hand-maintained `mutatingMethodNames` list (`MaxonBorrowCheck.maxon:42-44`) is not ported —
//     `Parser.arrayMethodMutatesReceiver` is shv2's ONE such list and it already exists.
//   • **NOTHING IS RECORDED UNTIL A FUNCTION ACTUALLY BORROWS.** Every mutation door reads
//     `borrows.count()` first, and a function with no live borrow answers 0 after one read and
//     allocates nothing — its `BorrowFacts` is still the shared empty. v1 rebuilds two arrays per
//     call site (`expireDeadBorrows`, :1099-1114) and the bootstrap allocates a key list on EVERY
//     op (`ExpireBorrows`, :164-171, with no empty-set guard); neither is ported. v1 is debugged,
//     not fast.
//
// ⚠ **WHAT IS NOT MODELLED, AND IT IS THE REFERENCES' LIMIT TOO: a LOOP makes source order lie.**
// A use textually BEFORE a mutation but inside the same loop body happens after it at run time,
// and a token-ordered last use cannot say so. Both references approximate with a linear op index
// and inherit exactly the same blind spot, so shv2 is no weaker; closing it needs a real liveness
// analysis over the CFG, which is a different mechanism from this one.

// The per-function id of a STORAGE a borrow reads an element of and a write goes through. Dense, and
// in practice tiny — it counts the DISTINCT such storages of one function body — but the range is WIDE
// because nothing CAPS it: a narrow bound here would be a runtime panic on a large machine-generated
// body rather than a diagnostic, and a bound is narrowed only when it is provable.
//
// ⭐ It is minted by resolving a NAME through `Scope` at the instant of the record, so it carries
// the two properties a name string cannot: a body that SHADOWS the source's name declares a
// different `VarInfo` and gets a different subject (correctly writable), and a self-field's two
// spellings (`items`, `self.items`) reach the ONE alias `VarInfo` and so share one subject.
export typealias BorrowSubjectId = int(0 to u64.max)

// The `via '<x>'` text for a mutation that is a REBIND of the source binding rather than a method
// call on it (`arr = other`, `items = other`, `self.items = other`). Neither reference has one —
// the bootstrap KILLS a borrow at a reassignment (`BorrowCheckPass.cs:129-132`, safe for it because
// it retains the element) — so shv2 names the write with the operator the user wrote.
export let ReassignMutationDisplay = b"="

// ⭐ **AN ELEMENT BORROW THE PARSER HAS MINTED BUT NOT YET BOUND TO A NAME.** A borrow is minted at
// the ACCESSOR (`arr.get(0)`) and completed at the BINDING (`let s = …`), which is where the
// borrowing name and the activation point exist; an accessor whose result no binding takes is never
// completed and is dropped.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS KEYED ON THE VALUE THE ACCESSOR PRODUCED, and that is not a detail — it is the only
// key that answers "did the element actually reach this name?".** Two measured defects come from
// keying on POSITION instead:
//
//   • `let n = sizeOf(try arr.get(0) otherwise "")` binds an INTEGER computed from the element. The
//     element reaches no name and nothing can dangle, but a position key hands the borrow to `n` and
//     refuses the program — as it does to a counted range's loop variable, which is the header phi.
//   • `let s = try arr.get(0) otherwise return 1` parses its handler by RE-ENTERING the statement
//     parser mid-initializer, so a position key that resets per statement is wiped there: NO borrow
//     is recorded at all and the following `arr.clear()` is waved through. Measured **0xC0000005**.
//
// A value id is unique within a function, so a stale pending borrow can never be claimed by a later
// binding and no per-statement reset is needed for correctness at all.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE COMPLETION IS WHAT MAKES A MUTATION INSIDE A `try … otherwise` HANDLER LEGAL**, and it
// is the one thing both references are emphatic about (`MbcBorrow.activationIndex`, v1 :425-428;
// `BorrowCheckPass.cs:72-80`). On the handler's path the get FAILED and the borrowing variable was
// never bound, so there is no borrow to conflict with — and since the handler is parsed BEFORE the
// binding completes, its tokens sit below the ACTIVATION and the ordering says so by construction.
// Activating at the CALL instead would refuse `specs/borrow-checker.md:borrow-not-live-in-otherwise`.
export type PendingBorrow
	export let subject as BorrowSubjectId
	// The SSA value the accessor defined. A binding claims this borrow iff the value it binds IS this
	// one — after `Parser.retargetPendingBorrow` has moved it to the merge phi, which is what a
	// `try … otherwise <value>` actually binds.
	export let borrowValue as ValueId
	// The accessor's own line — what the diagnostic reports as `borrowed at line N`.
	export let borrowLine as LineNumber

	export static function create(subject BorrowSubjectId, borrowValue ValueId, borrowLine LineNumber) returns PendingBorrow
		return Self{subject: subject, borrowValue: borrowValue, borrowLine: borrowLine}
	end 'create'
end 'PendingBorrow'

export typealias PendingBorrowArray = Array with PendingBorrow

// A completed borrow: binding `borrower` holds an element of subject `subject` from `activationToken`
// until its own last use.
export type BorrowRecord
	export let subject as BorrowSubjectId
	// ⭐ **THE BORROWING BINDING, HELD BY REFERENCE** — the same discipline
	// `Parser.iterationLockedBindings` states, and here for a second reason: the binding's
	// `lastUseToken` is written by `Parser.requireBindingLive` long AFTER this record is made, so a
	// name copied out at record time would be a second fact free to disagree with the binding it
	// describes. `VarInfo` is a heap `type`, so this record observes every later use.
	export let borrower as VarInfo
	// The token at which the borrowing name came into scope. A write BELOW it — a `try` handler's,
	// or anything earlier in the body — cannot conflict, because the borrow does not exist there yet.
	export let activationToken as TokenIndex
	export let borrowLine as LineNumber

	export static function create(subject BorrowSubjectId, borrower VarInfo, activationToken TokenIndex, borrowLine LineNumber) returns BorrowRecord
		return Self{subject: subject, borrower: borrower, activationToken: activationToken, borrowLine: borrowLine}
	end 'create'
end 'BorrowRecord'

export typealias BorrowRecordArray = Array with BorrowRecord

// A write the parser saw go through a borrow subject. Recorded ONLY while the enclosing function
// already holds at least one completed borrow — a write above every borrow's activation can never
// conflict, so recording one would be work with no question attached.
//
// ⚠ A record `type` rather than the dense scalar columns `MutationEdges` and `SourceRangeTable`
// insist on, and the gate above is exactly why: those two are built over EVERY call / EVERY op in
// the program, where a heap box per entry is a per-op allocation. This list is empty in every
// function that borrows nothing, which is almost all of them.
export type MutationSite
	export let subject as BorrowSubjectId
	// The name the SOURCE wrote at this write, which is what the diagnostic blames. It may differ
	// from the spelling the borrow used (`items` vs `self.items`) while naming the one subject.
	export let subjectName as ByteArray
	export let token as TokenIndex
	export let line as LineNumber
	export let column as ColumnNumber
	// The `via '<x>'` text: the METHOD name for a container write, the trimmed callee for a free
	// call, `=` for a rebind.
	export let display as ByteArray
	// ⭐ **NON-EMPTY ⇒ THIS SITE IS ONLY A WRITE IF THE CALLEE SAYS SO.** A container method and a
	// rebind are decided by the parser, which holds the whole question; `grow(arr)` is not — whether
	// it writes depends on `grow`'s BODY, possibly in another file. Those sites carry the callee and
	// the argument's column, and `mutationSiteWrites` settles them against the whole-program
	// `ParamMutationSummary` — the SAME summary E3019's call-site half reads, never a second one.
	export let callee as ByteArray
	// The argument's index in the call op's OWN columns (a prepended method receiver included), which
	// is the index `argSlotPosition` maps to a parameter position. Meaningless when `callee` is empty.
	export let argColumn as ParamCount
	export let argLabel as ByteArray

	export static function createContainerWrite(subject BorrowSubjectId, subjectName ByteArray, token TokenIndex, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber, display ByteArray) returns MutationSite
		return Self{subject: subject, subjectName: subjectName, token: token, line: line, column: column, display: display, callee: EmptySourceText, argColumn: 0, argLabel: EmptySourceText}
	end 'createContainerWrite'

	export static function createCallArgWrite(subject BorrowSubjectId, subjectName ByteArray, token TokenIndex, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber, callee ByteArray, argColumn ParamCount, argLabel ByteArray) returns MutationSite
		return Self{subject: subject, subjectName: subjectName, token: token, line: line, column: column, display: calleeDisplayName(callee), callee: callee, argColumn: argColumn, argLabel: argLabel}
	end 'createCallArgWrite'
end 'MutationSite'

export typealias MutationSiteArray = Array with MutationSite

// ⭐ **THE PER-FUNCTION BORROW FACTS — written by the PARSER, read HERE.** They ride on `IrFunction`
// for the reason `writtenParamMask` does: the parser holds the only view in which a binding exists,
// and the merged module is the only place the whole program does.
//
// ⚠ **A FUNCTION THAT BORROWS NOTHING SHARES ONE OBJECT** (`sharedEmptyBorrowFacts`) and allocates
// nothing at all, so every mutation door's `borrows.count() == 0` gate is one read against a
// permanently empty array. It is COPY-ON-WRITE (`Parser.borrowFactsOf`) and the shared instance is
// NEVER mutated — the same invariant `sharedEmptyIterationLocks` carries, and it must hold for the
// same reason: the per-file parse fan-out shares it across threads.
export type BorrowFacts
	// The distinct storages this body borrows from or writes, in first-seen order — the id space
	// `BorrowSubjectId` indexes. Parser working state; the resolver reads only its COUNT, as the key
	// space the borrow buckets are built over.
	export var subjects as VarInfoArray
	export var pending as PendingBorrowArray
	export var borrows as BorrowRecordArray
	export var mutations as MutationSiteArray

	export static function create() returns BorrowFacts
		return Self{subjects: VarInfoArray.create(), pending: PendingBorrowArray.create(), borrows: BorrowRecordArray.create(), mutations: MutationSiteArray.create()}
	end 'create'
end 'BorrowFacts'

// The one instance every function starts at — see `BorrowFacts`'s ⚠ block for the invariant.
export let sharedEmptyBorrowFacts = BorrowFacts.create()

// The `via '<x>'` text for a FREE call: the callee's UNQUALIFIED name, so `Bag.wipe` reads `wipe` and
// a bare `grow` is unchanged. v1's `formatCalleeDisplay` (`MaxonBorrowCheck.maxon:1059-1076`) trims
// the same way.
//
// ⚠ It goes through `splitQualifiedName`, the tree's ONE home for "where does a qualified name
// divide". A hand-rolled scan here would be a FOURTH spelling of the separator, beside
// `QualifiedAliasSeparatorByte`, `containsQualifier` and `Parser.MethodNameSeparatorBytes` — and a
// divergence between them would be a wrong string in a diagnostic, not a compile error.
//
// ⚠ The bootstrap trims after the FIRST dot instead (`FormatMethodName`, :332-335), which on its own
// namespaced names yields `StringArray.push` — a divergence with no corpus case either way, because
// the only qualified callee that can mutate a borrow subject is a static taking the array as an
// ordinary argument. shv2 follows v1: the unqualified name is what the user reads at the call.
function calleeDisplayName(callee ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	return match splitQualifiedName(callee) 'display'
		parts(_, member) gives member
		notQualified gives callee
	end 'display'
end 'calleeDisplayName'

// The E3070 wire text, in ONE place. Byte-identical to both reference compilers' — the four
// `maxoncstderr` goldens this rung enables pin every word of it.
function borrowConflictMessage(subjectName ByteArray, display ByteArray, borrowerName ByteArray, borrowLine LineNumber) returns String
	return "cannot mutate '{String.from(subjectName)}' via '{String.from(display)}' while it is borrowed by '{String.from(borrowerName)}' (borrowed at line {borrowLine})"
end 'borrowConflictMessage'

// ⭐ **THE ONE RESOLVER.** Every E3070 in the program comes out of `reportFirstLiveBorrow` below, so
// there is exactly one message format and one anchor rule however the write was spelled.
//
// Run from `semanticCheck`, and it must be: the free-call half needs the whole-program
// `ParamMutationSummary`, which does not exist until every file is parsed. It adds no `CompilePhase`
// for that reason too — the timing tables and `docs/optimization-log.md` stay comparable.
export function checkBorrowConflicts(project Project, module MaxonModule, mutationSummary ParamMutationSummary)
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		checkFunctionBorrowConflicts(project, func: func, mutationSummary: mutationSummary)
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'checkBorrowConflicts'

function checkFunctionBorrowConflicts(project Project, func IrFunction, mutationSummary ParamMutationSummary)
	let facts = func.borrowFacts
	if facts.borrows.count() == 0 'borrowsNothing'
		return
	end 'borrowsNothing'

	// ⭐ **THE BORROWS, BUCKETED BY SUBJECT — built ONCE per function, never per site.** A site then
	// walks only the borrows of the storage it writes, instead of every borrow in the body. The
	// bucket order is the borrows' own append order, which is activation order, which is what makes
	// "blame the FIRST active borrow" (v1 :1118-1123) a property of the program rather than of a
	// map's iteration: `multiple-refs-survive-array-growth` blames `'a'`, not `'c'`.
	//
	// Both references instead REBUILD their active set at every op — the bootstrap allocates a key
	// list per op (`ExpireBorrows`, :164-171) and v1 two arrays per call site (`expireDeadBorrows`,
	// :1099-1114). Nothing is allocated per site here.
	var subjectKeys = CsrColumn.create()
	var borrowRows = CsrColumn.create()
	for i in 0 upto facts.borrows.count() 'eachBorrow'
		subjectKeys.push(borrowAt(facts, index: i).subject)
		borrowRows.push(i)
	end 'eachBorrow'
	let bySubject = buildCsr(subjectKeys, to: borrowRows, nodeCount: facts.subjects.count())

	for s in 0 upto facts.mutations.count() 'eachSite'
		let site = try facts.mutations.get(s) otherwise panic("checkFunctionBorrowConflicts: mutations.get OOB at {s}")
		if not mutationSiteWrites(project, site: site, mutationSummary: mutationSummary) 'notAWriteAfterAll'
			continue
		end 'notAWriteAfterAll'

		reportFirstLiveBorrow(project, func: func, facts: facts, bySubject: bySubject, site: site)
	end 'eachSite'
end 'checkFunctionBorrowConflicts'

// Does this recorded site actually WRITE its subject? A container method and a rebind were settled
// by the parser, which held the whole question. A call ARGUMENT was not: it writes iff the callee
// writes the parameter it fills, which is what the whole-program summary answers — and it is
// answered through the SAME `argSlotPosition` bridge `checkImmutableArgToMutatingParam` uses, so
// the source-order column and the declaration-order mask are never bridged twice.
function mutationSiteWrites(project Project, site MutationSite, mutationSummary ParamMutationSummary) returns bool
	if site.callee.isEmpty() 'settledAtTheParse'
		return true
	end 'settledAtTheParse'

	let mask = mutationSummary.maskOf(site.callee)
	if mask == NoMutatedParams 'writesNoParameter'
		return false
	end 'writesNoParameter'

	// An undeclared callee is E3004 from `validateCall`; there is no signature to slot against and no
	// second diagnostic to add here.
	let sig = try project.funcSignatures.get(site.callee) otherwise return false
	let pos = argSlotPosition(sig, argIndex: site.argColumn, label: site.argLabel)
	return pos < sig.paramCount() and paramIsMutated(mask, param: pos)
end 'mutationSiteWrites'

// Report the FIRST borrow of this site's subject that is already active and not yet dead, and stop —
// one diagnostic per write, naming one borrow, exactly as both references do. Anchored on the WRITE,
// which is the line:col the four goldens pin.
function reportFirstLiveBorrow(project Project, func IrFunction, facts BorrowFacts, bySubject CsrGraph, site MutationSite)
	for k in 0 upto bySubject.degree(site.subject) 'eachBorrowOfThisSubject'
		let borrow = borrowAt(facts, index: bySubject.neighbour(site.subject, k: k))

		// NOT YET BOUND at this write (a `try` handler's, or anything earlier in the body): the
		// borrowing name does not exist there, so there is nothing to conflict with.
		if borrow.activationToken > site.token 'notBorrowedYet'
			continue
		end 'notBorrowedYet'

		// NON-LEXICAL LIFETIMES: the borrow ends at the borrowing binding's LAST USE, not at its
		// scope's end. `lastUseToken` is seeded to the activation, so a binding never read again is
		// dead the instant it is bound — which is what makes `borrow-nll-allowed` compile.
		if borrow.borrower.lastUseToken <= site.token 'borrowAlreadyDead'
			continue
		end 'borrowAlreadyDead'

		reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.semanticBorrowConflict, message: borrowConflictMessage(site.subjectName, display: site.display, borrowerName: borrow.borrower.name, borrowLine: borrow.borrowLine), line: site.line, column: site.column, filePath: func.sourceFilePath)
		return
	end 'eachBorrowOfThisSubject'
end 'reportFirstLiveBorrow'

// The one indexed read of the borrow column, so the two walks above share its bound message.
function borrowAt(facts BorrowFacts, index CsrIndex) returns BorrowRecord
	return try facts.borrows.get(index) otherwise panic("BorrowCheck: borrows.get OOB at {index} — the subject buckets are built over this very column")
end 'borrowAt'
